    "domain": "study_forensics",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "task_type": "study_forensics",
    "prompt": "In an open-access randomised controlled trial, 89 adults diagnosed with autism practiced mindfulness using a smartphone app for six weeks. Compared with a wait-list control group, the intervention group reported significant reductions in anxiety and perceived stress; when the control group later used the app, they replicated the improvements. Gains persisted six weeks after practice stopped. Lead investigator John Gabrieli said he and his colleagues were already convinced the app could help autistic adults and suggested it might benefit people of all ages.\n",
    "context": "Although the trial design is solid and results are promising, the lead investigator's assertion\nthat mindfulness apps will help autistic adults and potentially everyone else goes beyond the\navailable evidence.\n",
    "expected_answer": "overclaim",
    "reference_solution": "Although the trial design is solid and results are promising, the lead investigator's assertion that mindfulness apps will help autistic adults and potentially everyone else goes beyond the available evidence, making this an overclaim case.\n",
